          "reasoning_summary": "The route reads an attacker-controlled query parameter and passes it through a string-concatenation wrapper into an explicit /bin/sh -c invocation. Per the command_injection knowledge card, untrusted text reaching the command-string slot of an explicit sh -c is shell injection. A payload such as name=\";id;\" or name=$(id) executes arbitrary commands. No guard exists at any layer.",
          "false_positive_analysis": "Not a false positive: the sink is an explicit shell invocation (['/bin/sh','-c',command]), not an argv-preserving call, so shell metacharacters are interpreted. The concatenation is direct with no escaping. The only theoretical mitigation would be a sanitizer, which is absent. This is not mere argument injection; it is full shell command injection.",
          "remediation": "Avoid the shell entirely: use subprocess.check_output(['printf', 'report for ' + name], text=True) with an argument list and shell=False. If a shell is unavoidable, pass untrusted data via environment variables or stdin, never interpolated into the command string.",
